From: Velocor <noreply@velocor.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] bdev/lvol: add bdev_lvol_get_fragmap

Incremental backups need the blocks that changed between two snapshots. An lvol snapshot owns
exactly the clusters written while it was the newest layer of its chain (the rest it reads
through from its parent), so the changes between two snapshots of a chain are the clusters the
layers after the older one own, up to the newer one. There is no RPC that reports which
clusters an lvol owns.

Add bdev_lvol_get_fragmap, the RPC of Longhorn's SPDK fork (github.com/longhorn/spdk, commit
3e764f556 "fragmap: implement bdev_lvol_get_fragmap" by Derek Su), with the same parameters
(name, offset, size) and result (cluster_size, num_clusters, num_allocated_clusters, fragmap: a
base64 bitmap, cluster i at bit i % 8 of byte i / 8), so tools written for it work unchanged.

Differences from that implementation:
- the map is read from the blob's cluster table with spdk_blob_get_next_allocated_io_unit() /
  spdk_blob_get_next_unallocated_io_unit(), the functions the lvol bdev's SEEK_DATA/SEEK_HOLE
  use, so the result is the same, but synchronously and without a bdev descriptor, an I/O
  channel and two seek I/Os per extent (whose completion status the original didn't check);
- size 0 means "to the end of the lvol" also with a non-zero offset (the original then mapped
  past the end);
- cluster sizes needn't be powers of two;
- the bitmap is encoded in the lvol module, without a new lib/util function.

Also: the schema entry, an rpc.py command and the RPC documentation.

Unit test: ut_lvol_get_fragmap.

Applies to SPDK v26.09 (0bbb7fe4dccfb31e8d446da8822360704949d1e9) after 0001-0004.
